<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Windows manifest. It exists for ONE line: activeCodePage.

  Windows has a "narrow" half of its API (files, console, third-party libraries
  taking char*), and by default it reads bytes in the system ANSI code page:
  CP1251 on a Russian machine, CP1252 on a Western one. Our strings are UTF-8
  everywhere. So any crossing of the narrow half (QString::toLocal8Bit(),
  TIFFOpen, printf with Russian text) silently lost Cyrillic, turning it into
  question marks.

  Caught in practice: the test-suite harness passes the output directory
  through toLocal8Bit()/fromLocal8Bit(), and a path with a six-letter Cyrillic
  directory name (".../<tests dir>/zoom") came out as ".../??????/zoom" on the
  other side. Snapshots were written into that same directory, the suite could
  not find them, and eleven suites went red through no fault of their own.

  Starting with Windows 10 1903 a program may declare that its ANSI code page
  is UTF-8. Then the narrow half stops being narrow: GetACP() returns 65001,
  and every crossing becomes lossless at once. Verified: under wine GetACP() is
  1252 without the manifest and 65001 with it.

  On Windows older than 1903 the line is simply ignored: not a failure, a
  fallback to the previous behaviour. That is why the places where a path goes
  to the file system still use the wide calls (writeNewFile, TIFFOpenW): the
  manifest is the top layer of protection, not the only one.

  dpiAware/dpiAwareness are deliberately NOT declared: Qt 6 sets DPI awareness
  itself, programmatically, and anything declared here would conflict with it.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32" name="org.zametti.zametti" version="1.0.0.0"/>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings xmlns:ws2019="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">
      <ws2019:activeCodePage>UTF-8</ws2019:activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
